00:01:42.034 --> 00:01:45.167
- [Voiceover] Jelle asks, "what
came first at VaynerMedia:

00:01:45.167 --> 00:01:47.314
clients or employees?

00:01:47.314 --> 00:01:50.056
And did you ever do
stuff without employees?"

00:01:50.838 --> 00:01:51.825
- So let's get into this question.

00:01:51.825 --> 00:01:53.705
First, I actually don't
know how to pronounce this,

00:01:53.705 --> 00:01:56.192
so let's go to India, who I
thought helped us with that,

00:01:56.192 --> 00:01:58.143
DRock, how - ?

00:01:58.143 --> 00:02:00.019
- I don't - "yell"? "Yelle"? I don't know.

00:02:00.019 --> 00:02:01.363
- [Gary] Got it, alright.

00:02:01.363 --> 00:02:03.034
Steve? What's your shot here?

00:02:03.034 --> 00:02:04.234
- "Yella?" I don't know.

00:02:04.234 --> 00:02:05.535
- You know.
- [India] It's very pretty.

00:02:05.535 --> 00:02:06.624
- It's gorgeous. Yeah, don't worry,

00:02:06.624 --> 00:02:07.887
you're not hurting feelings anyway.

00:02:07.887 --> 00:02:09.687
India's going to be very
sensitive on this show.

00:02:09.687 --> 00:02:11.884
(laughter)
Alright, you know,

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:14.757
we started Vaynermedia, two
things happened in parallel.

00:02:14.757 --> 00:02:17.636
We got out, I got ahead of
it, AJ was graduating college

00:02:17.636 --> 00:02:19.691
and we were gonna start a company in May.

00:02:19.691 --> 00:02:21.382
In March or April I got ahead of it

00:02:21.382 --> 00:02:24.298
and got us a big project
with a big client.

00:02:24.298 --> 00:02:27.303
So I guess customer came first.

00:02:27.303 --> 00:02:29.242
I kinda used, I did something clever.

00:02:29.242 --> 00:02:32.272
I made that person pay the
entire project up front

00:02:32.272 --> 00:02:35.256
and then used those dollars
to pay the first 3-4 employees

00:02:35.256 --> 00:02:37.716
who were all of AJ's homies,

00:02:37.716 --> 00:02:41.652
who are all still here,
hence the foundation.

00:02:41.652 --> 00:02:42.813
So I guess client, right?

00:02:42.813 --> 00:02:46.551
I got an upfront campaign
that I used those dollars,

00:02:46.551 --> 00:02:47.817
and then the official first day

00:02:47.817 --> 00:02:52.053
we had those five or six employees, so

00:02:52.066 --> 00:02:54.810
but we never did anything without them,

00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:57.829
though actually me and AJ
did some of the early stuff

00:02:57.829 --> 00:03:01.671
for that project by ourselves, so

00:03:01.671 --> 00:03:02.670
that's the answer.

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:03.794
I don't know how you wanna look at it.

00:03:03.794 --> 00:03:04.771
I will say this:

00:03:04.771 --> 00:03:06.571
any time you can sell
ahead of your expenses,

00:03:06.571 --> 00:03:07.428
you do it.

00:03:07.428 --> 00:03:08.347
One of the biggest reasons

00:03:08.347 --> 00:03:09.449
so many people go out of business,

00:03:09.449 --> 00:03:11.527
and many of you who watch this
show will go out of business,

00:03:11.527 --> 00:03:13.907
is you do not know how
to manage cash flow.

00:03:13.907 --> 00:03:16.531
You think in terms of, you know,

00:03:16.531 --> 00:03:19.967
accrual versus cash basis,
if we wanna go hardcore,

00:03:19.967 --> 00:03:21.453
you know, CPA-style here,

00:03:21.453 --> 00:03:25.064
oh, we're gonna make 80k so
I can have 70k in expenses,

00:03:25.064 --> 00:03:27.629
but if you don't get paid
properly or if there's a hiccup,

00:03:27.629 --> 00:03:29.173
or, you know, no buffers,

00:03:29.173 --> 00:03:32.749
no practical knowledge
of that vulnerability,

00:03:32.749 --> 00:03:35.012
and then a bunch of you
who are tech-driven,

00:03:35.012 --> 00:03:39.226
you raise too much money, you
don't keep your burn in check,

00:03:39.226 --> 00:03:40.724
you assume you're gonna raise more money,

00:03:40.724 --> 00:03:42.952
it doesn't go as easy
or as well as you think,

00:03:42.952 --> 00:03:44.346
because once you actually become a company

00:03:44.346 --> 00:03:46.169
people are looking at what you're doing

00:03:46.169 --> 00:03:48.293
versus what you promise you're gonna do,

00:03:48.293 --> 00:03:49.569
and those are the vulnerabilities

00:03:49.569 --> 00:03:51.693
of how you go out of business.

00:03:51.973 --> 00:03:56.070
- [Voiceover] Gabii asks, "Do
you have any bucket list items

00:03:56.070 --> 00:03:58.827
that you want to complete before 40?"

00:03:59.658 --> 00:04:01.260
- Gabii, this is a good question,

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:02.596
one that I was excited to answer

00:04:02.596 --> 00:04:04.232
mainly because the truth is,

00:04:04.232 --> 00:04:07.738
I only have one bucket list
item in my life professionally,

00:04:07.738 --> 00:04:11.697
and that is to buy the New York Jets,

00:04:11.697 --> 00:04:14.378
and thus I don't have any bucket
list items professionally.

00:04:14.378 --> 00:04:17.641
Personally, I don't want to
climb any goddamn mountains,

00:04:17.641 --> 00:04:19.950
or jump out of anything.
I'm not that dude,

00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:21.216
so I don't care about that,

00:04:21.216 --> 00:04:23.689
the bucket list item, you
know, on my personal life

00:04:23.689 --> 00:04:25.070
is quite basic as well,

00:04:25.070 --> 00:04:27.334
which is, I just wanna love my family,

00:04:27.334 --> 00:04:29.539
and I'm executing on that
and accomplishing that,

00:04:29.539 --> 00:04:31.676
and so the answer is no,

00:04:31.676 --> 00:04:35.909
not before 40 or 50 or
60 or 70 or 80 or 90,

00:04:35.909 --> 00:04:38.375
and the truth is, and this may actually

00:04:38.375 --> 00:04:40.151
lead to why I'm so happy,

00:04:40.151 --> 00:04:43.030
the two bucket list items in my life is

00:04:43.030 --> 00:04:47.894
to love and cherish and
make my family proud,

00:04:47.894 --> 00:04:52.351
and to live the process of
buying the New York Jets

00:04:52.351 --> 00:04:54.628
more than buying the New York Jets.

00:04:54.628 --> 00:04:56.973
And I've actually accomplished both.

00:04:56.973 --> 00:04:58.111
Which puts me in a good mood.

00:04:58.111 --> 00:05:01.079
- Gary, is the high-end wine
business just a complete hoax?

00:05:01.079 --> 00:05:02.467
(laughter)

00:05:02.467 --> 00:05:04.620
Does anyone actually ever taste the oak?

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:06.406
What does oak taste like?

00:05:06.406 --> 00:05:09.602
- That's a great question, Steph.

00:05:10.907 --> 00:05:12.870
Is it a hoax?

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:16.856
It's as much of a hoax
as the art world is,

00:05:17.246 --> 00:05:21.355
it's as much of a hoax
as a high-end restaurant

00:05:21.355 --> 00:05:24.745
that charges, you know, $10,000 a head

00:05:24.745 --> 00:05:27.148
for a once-in-a-lifetime meal.

00:05:27.148 --> 00:05:30.631
It is the classic example of
supply and demand, my dear.

00:05:30.631 --> 00:05:33.952
And coming from a, you know,
business-oriented TV show

00:05:33.952 --> 00:05:35.484
and living in the world that you're in,

00:05:35.484 --> 00:05:39.164
is a stock price a hoax?

00:05:39.164 --> 00:05:41.427
The answer is, yes and no.

00:05:41.427 --> 00:05:44.272
To me, it's not a hoax, because
I live a very basic life

00:05:44.272 --> 00:05:45.770
of supply and demand.

00:05:45.770 --> 00:05:49.089
I think when I get paid to
speak onstage is a hoax,

00:05:49.089 --> 00:05:52.170
and I did for awhile until I
realized, well, not really,

00:05:52.170 --> 00:05:55.095
because I'm compensated for
what I do for that event.

00:05:55.095 --> 00:05:56.639
And so, you know,

00:05:56.639 --> 00:05:59.495
does an actor who gets
paid $8 million for a movie

00:05:59.495 --> 00:06:01.317
but never gets brought
up, like an athlete,

00:06:01.317 --> 00:06:03.337
for being overpaid, is that a hoax?

00:06:03.337 --> 00:06:05.195
Yes and no.

00:06:05.195 --> 00:06:06.216
When you start understanding

00:06:06.216 --> 00:06:08.248
that actor brings people into the theater,

00:06:08.248 --> 00:06:09.328
or makes people watch,

00:06:09.328 --> 00:06:11.371
and then they sell advertising against it.

00:06:11.371 --> 00:06:12.938
It's all an arbitrage.

00:06:12.938 --> 00:06:16.595
And so the wine world,
back to the basic question,

00:06:16.595 --> 00:06:18.174
can you taste differences in wine?

00:06:18.174 --> 00:06:20.008
Absolutely. I live it.

00:06:20.008 --> 00:06:21.773
I mean, I can taste the difference

00:06:21.773 --> 00:06:25.372
between an $80 wine and a
$10 wine pretty consistently.

00:06:25.372 --> 00:06:27.531
Can I taste the difference
between the $1000 wine

00:06:27.531 --> 00:06:29.295
and a $500 wine,

00:06:29.295 --> 00:06:31.396
or a $1000 wine and a $100 wine?

00:06:31.396 --> 00:06:32.546
A lot of times, no.

00:06:32.546 --> 00:06:35.030
The beauty of wine is,
everybody's got their own palette,

00:06:35.030 --> 00:06:37.445
back to art or music, right?

00:06:37.445 --> 00:06:40.510
Is it a hoax that an EDM DJ

00:06:40.510 --> 00:06:44.550
gets a ton of people
into a place in Sweden?

00:06:44.550 --> 00:06:46.733
For me, well, actually,
I've gotten more into EDM,

00:06:46.733 --> 00:06:48.868
but, like, theoretically it is.

00:06:48.868 --> 00:06:50.227
But, you know, and that's it.

00:06:50.227 --> 00:06:52.352
It's brand, it's supply and demand,

00:06:52.352 --> 00:06:53.674
it's how the game works,

00:06:53.674 --> 00:06:56.367
and so the answer is, I
don't think it's a hoax,

00:06:56.367 --> 00:06:59.153
but if you're watching this show right now

00:06:59.153 --> 00:07:01.347
and you don't appreciate the game,

00:07:01.347 --> 00:07:02.682
so here's a good piece of advice,

00:07:02.682 --> 00:07:03.844
let's get into real good advice.

00:07:03.844 --> 00:07:06.855
Don't drink good wine.

00:07:06.855 --> 00:07:08.167
Let me say that one more time.

00:07:08.167 --> 00:07:10.836
Don't put yourself in a position

00:07:10.836 --> 00:07:13.622
to understand why it's not a hoax.

00:07:14.915 --> 00:07:17.540
Don't sit first class,

00:07:17.540 --> 00:07:19.340
because then you realize it's not a hoax,

00:07:19.340 --> 00:07:22.358
because you like it better
than the middle seat in coach.

00:07:22.358 --> 00:07:25.434
Don't get front-row seats to a ballgame,

00:07:25.434 --> 00:07:26.932
then you understand that it's not a hoax

00:07:26.932 --> 00:07:28.894
compared to sitting in the upper deck.

00:07:28.894 --> 00:07:30.798
It's context, baby.

00:07:30.798 --> 00:07:33.131
And so the bottom line
- that made me happy.

00:07:33.131 --> 00:07:34.256
(laughter)

00:07:34.256 --> 00:07:36.115
The bottom line is, it's all perspective,

00:07:36.115 --> 00:07:37.877
it's all supply and demand,

00:07:37.877 --> 00:07:39.330
but that's what it comes down to,

00:07:39.330 --> 00:07:40.712
you start understanding

00:07:40.712 --> 00:07:43.660
why these things fetch the
dollar amounts they fetch

00:07:43.660 --> 00:07:45.355
when you actually taste it,

00:07:45.355 --> 00:07:47.701
versus you theoretically
complain about it.

00:07:48.561 --> 00:07:49.746
- [Voiceover] Cédric asks,

00:07:49.746 --> 00:07:52.593
"How do you not procrastinate that well?"

00:07:54.103 --> 00:07:55.798
- Cédric.

00:07:55.798 --> 00:07:56.762
It's unbelievable how well

00:07:56.762 --> 00:07:59.118
Cedric the Entertainer branded himself,

00:07:59.118 --> 00:08:00.059
because I literally was about to

00:08:00.059 --> 00:08:01.940
call Cédric here the Entertainer.

00:08:01.940 --> 00:08:03.925
Cédric, I'm calling you the Entertainer.

00:08:03.925 --> 00:08:05.538
As a matter of fact, I want to make

00:08:05.538 --> 00:08:08.336
a little piece of content for Cédric

00:08:08.336 --> 00:08:10.241
and I'll tweet it out.

00:08:10.241 --> 00:08:11.423
Cédric, you won.

00:08:11.423 --> 00:08:13.862
I need a little "Cédric
is the Entertainer,"

00:08:13.862 --> 00:08:17.054
take his Twitter profile,
and we're gonna make him.

00:08:17.054 --> 00:08:18.854
This is a new thing we can
do on the #AskGaryVee Show.

00:08:18.854 --> 00:08:21.624
We could make things for
fans, one-off t-shirts,

00:08:21.624 --> 00:08:23.958
pieces of content, I'm
seeing something here.

00:08:23.958 --> 00:08:25.479
This is gonna make a lot more people

00:08:25.479 --> 00:08:27.441
ask a lot more questions.

00:08:27.441 --> 00:08:29.960
Cédric, here's a curveball,

00:08:29.960 --> 00:08:33.280
I actually think I'm an
obnoxious procrastinator,

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:35.380
while equally not being.

00:08:35.380 --> 00:08:36.764
Meaning

00:08:36.764 --> 00:08:39.897
I stay in constant audit mode.

00:08:39.897 --> 00:08:43.251
Can I get a constant
audit mode alert here?

00:08:43.251 --> 00:08:44.577
By the way, I have to
watch yesterday's episode

00:08:44.577 --> 00:08:45.679
to see what you did with the alerts.

00:08:45.679 --> 00:08:47.436
Yesterday had a lot of editing.
I gotta watch it outright.

00:08:47.436 --> 00:08:48.886
I don't watch my stuff, by the way.

00:08:48.886 --> 00:08:50.465
It's a little fun fact for all of you.

00:08:50.465 --> 00:08:54.722
Sorry, DRock, Staphon,
get to see the great work.

00:08:55.331 --> 00:08:57.735
There's a ton of stuff
that I procrastinate,

00:08:57.735 --> 00:08:59.568
and I think I'm a procrastinator,

00:08:59.568 --> 00:09:02.632
but what I think I also am is

00:09:02.632 --> 00:09:05.013
always leveling up
whatever's most important

00:09:05.013 --> 00:09:07.613
and prioritizing it in real time.

00:09:07.613 --> 00:09:09.191
Team can tell you here,

00:09:09.191 --> 00:09:12.094
I bet you Steve's favorite inside joke is

00:09:12.094 --> 00:09:13.754
DeMayo.

00:09:14.188 --> 00:09:14.908
Can somebody get me the,

00:09:14.908 --> 00:09:16.858
literally, get me DeMayo?

00:09:18.430 --> 00:09:19.869
Watch this.

00:09:19.869 --> 00:09:23.897
This will answer your question
perfectly, VaynerNation,

00:09:24.195 --> 00:09:26.438
because the truth is,

00:09:26.842 --> 00:09:28.244
I do procrastinate,

00:09:28.244 --> 00:09:31.088
but I'm adjusting to
the reality of my life

00:09:31.088 --> 00:09:32.782
at the moment I'm living it,

00:09:32.782 --> 00:09:37.008
so if something is
super-important yesterday,

00:09:37.008 --> 00:09:40.827
I can literally decide that
it's less important in an hour,

00:09:40.827 --> 00:09:43.266
predicated on what comes into my inbox,

00:09:43.266 --> 00:09:45.610
or the meeting that I'm about
to have right after this.

00:09:45.610 --> 00:09:48.291
And so the reason I think
it feels like I'm not,

00:09:48.291 --> 00:09:50.348
and so much is getting accomplished,

00:09:50.348 --> 00:09:52.889
is my pants are on fire

00:09:52.889 --> 00:09:55.362
for the thing that I deem most important

00:09:55.362 --> 00:09:56.825
at this exact moment.

00:09:56.825 --> 00:09:59.529
And that is how it works over and over,

00:09:59.529 --> 00:10:00.264
where's DeMayo?

00:10:00.264 --> 00:10:01.519
And over. I'm trying to stall here.

00:10:01.519 --> 00:10:03.968
And over, and over, and over again.

00:10:03.968 --> 00:10:06.961
Let's go to the next question,
we'll get back to DeMayo.

00:10:08.091 --> 00:10:09.043
- [Man offscreen] Oh, here he is.

00:10:09.043 --> 00:10:11.063
- Oh, here we go. Don't go, DRock.

00:10:11.063 --> 00:10:13.098
Leave all that. Don't
edit that, I'll be pissed.

00:10:13.098 --> 00:10:15.826
Now, tell the VaynerNation how--

00:10:15.826 --> 00:10:16.778
- What's up, world.

00:10:16.778 --> 00:10:21.143
- Tell the VaynerNation
how often I'll send an email

00:10:21.143 --> 00:10:23.999
that will say "now, this is top priority"

00:10:23.999 --> 00:10:25.787
versus the next day, this is top priority,

00:10:25.787 --> 00:10:28.621
and then you get crippled
by the notion of,

00:10:28.621 --> 00:10:29.385
hold on.
(laughter)

00:10:29.385 --> 00:10:30.883
If, like, literally, when I'm like, no no,

00:10:30.883 --> 00:10:32.754
this is the #1 priority in my life.

00:10:32.754 --> 00:10:34.633
- No, tippy-top priority
is the way you always say.

00:10:34.633 --> 00:10:35.911
- Tippy-top? Tippy-top.

00:10:35.911 --> 00:10:37.339
- TIppy-top priority.

00:10:37.339 --> 00:10:39.080
Yeah, probably every
time you're on a flight,

00:10:39.080 --> 00:10:41.355
there's probably about 15 emails
that come after the flight.

00:10:41.355 --> 00:10:42.771
- And then you struggle
